1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,916
A list of phone numbers is never fully clean.

2
00:00:03,916 --> 00:00:08,090
This video shows how the platform completes a call to a dead number

3
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:12,036
on its own, keeps that number out of every later load and dial,

4
00:00:12,036 --> 00:00:13,980
and how you manage the list.

5
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:25,016
Some numbers in a list are disconnected, some were never valid, and some have changed hands.

6
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:28,760
Calling them wastes agent time and dialer capacity.

7
00:00:29,510 --> 00:00:34,430
When a call reaches one of them, the network says the number is not in service.

8
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:39,625
The platform then completes the attempt on its own, and adds the number

9
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:44,300
to the Numbers Not In Service list, so no campaign loads or dials it again.

10
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:53,377
This works the same way for the Preview, Power and Progressive dialers, and

11
00:00:53,377 --> 00:00:55,123
for automatic AI loads.

12
00:00:55,123 --> 00:01:00,000
The call is placed, and the carrier answers that the number does not exist.

13
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:02,688
No agent is connected.

14
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:07,810
An automatic call never starts the AI conversation or the AI review.

15
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:13,004
The activity is completed with the subject's not-in-service disposition.

16
00:01:13,004 --> 00:01:16,480
Its notes record the number, and what the carrier said.

17
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:22,055
The number is added to the list, with the campaign and the activity that found it.

18
00:01:22,055 --> 00:01:26,277
The activity is not retried, and the contact's history keeps it, so you

19
00:01:26,277 --> 00:01:28,580
can see the dialer tried the number.

20
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:33,122
Some carriers answer the call and play a recording instead.

21
00:01:33,122 --> 00:01:38,490
Those calls look answered, so the agent picks your not-in-service disposition, and the

22
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:40,590
number is marked the same way.

23
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:49,293
The platform uses a disposition with the outcome Number not in service.

24
00:01:49,293 --> 00:01:51,446
Open Dispositions, and find it.

25
00:01:51,446 --> 00:01:54,413
The badge next to its name shows the outcome.

26
00:01:54,869 --> 00:01:59,721
Add it from Add Disposition by setting its Outcome to Number not in service.

27
00:01:59,721 --> 00:02:04,395
If you add no such disposition, the platform creates one named Number Not

28
00:02:04,395 --> 00:02:07,058
In Service the first time it needs one.

29
00:02:07,058 --> 00:02:12,029
When an agent picks a disposition with this outcome, the number is marked too.

30
00:02:16,229 --> 00:02:20,885
When activities are loaded, the next number of the contact is used instead,

31
00:02:20,885 --> 00:02:22,322
in the usual order.

32
00:02:22,322 --> 00:02:26,519
A contact whose numbers are all on the list is skipped, and counted

33
00:02:26,519 --> 00:02:29,049
as having only numbers not in service.

34
00:02:29,799 --> 00:02:33,703
In the dialer, a record whose number was added after it was loaded

35
00:02:33,703 --> 00:02:35,819
is cancelled before it is dialed.

36
00:02:36,569 --> 00:02:42,279
In automatic loads, the activity is cancelled, instead of called or texted.

37
00:02:43,029 --> 00:02:47,637
On the contact card, the number shows a red Not in service badge.

38
00:02:47,637 --> 00:02:49,649
Hover over it to see why.

39
00:02:53,857 --> 00:02:58,496
Open Interaction Center, Management, Numbers Not In Service.

40
00:02:58,496 --> 00:03:03,697
The list is empty until a call, a lookup or a person marks a number.

41
00:03:04,154 --> 00:03:07,415
Mark as not in service adds a number by hand.

42
00:03:07,415 --> 00:03:11,954
Enter it with its country code, and click Mark as not in service.

43
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:15,019
The number is listed with what found it.

44
00:03:15,019 --> 00:03:19,000
Here that is a person, and the list shows when it was last found.

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,579
Two more numbers are added the same way.

46
00:03:22,145 --> 00:03:25,543
Search by number filters the list by digits.

47
00:03:25,543 --> 00:03:27,445
Press Enter to search.

48
00:03:27,895 --> 00:03:32,967
Allow dialing removes the mark, so campaigns can load and dial the number again.

49
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:37,390
Use it when the number is back in service or belongs to somebody new.

50
00:03:37,390 --> 00:03:39,395
It does not call the number.

51
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:45,606
To allow dialing for several numbers, select them, and pick Actions, Allow dialing.

52
00:03:49,806 --> 00:03:56,632
With phone number verification turned on, each contact's preferred number is checked by a lookup provider.

53
00:03:56,632 --> 00:04:01,020
A number the lookup reports as invalid, or as an inactive line, is

54
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:03,946
added to the list before anybody dials it.

55
00:04:04,696 --> 00:04:10,046
A number reported as unreachable, switched off or out of coverage, is not added.

56
00:04:10,796 --> 00:04:14,850
If a later lookup finds the same number active again, the mark the

57
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:19,073
lookup made is removed, because the number was given to somebody new.

58
00:04:19,073 --> 00:04:22,846
A mark made by a real call stays until you use Allow dialing.

59
00:04:27,046 --> 00:04:31,837
The Campaign summary and Subject inventory reports have a Not in service column.

60
00:04:31,837 --> 00:04:36,963
It counts activities that were dialed and found dead, and activities cancelled because

61
00:04:36,963 --> 00:04:39,526
the number was already known to be dead.

62
00:04:40,276 --> 00:04:44,052
Use it to see how clean a campaign's list was, and how much

63
00:04:44,052 --> 00:04:46,086
dialing went on dead numbers.

64
00:04:50,286 --> 00:04:54,872
Before a big campaign, a manager marks numbers the team already knows are

65
00:04:54,872 --> 00:04:57,776
dead, so they are skipped on the first load.

66
00:04:58,526 --> 00:05:02,549
A number that has been given to somebody new is released with Allow

67
00:05:02,549 --> 00:05:05,416
dialing, so campaigns can reach the new owner.

68
00:05:06,166 --> 00:05:10,781
Each campaign reports how many of its numbers were dead, which shows whether

69
00:05:10,781 --> 00:05:13,146
a purchased list is worth its price.

70
00:05:15,146 --> 00:05:19,165
Let the platform mark dead numbers, keep the list clean, and release a

71
00:05:19,165 --> 00:05:21,280
number when it is back in service.

72
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,656
Thanks for watching.

